[NIST SP 800-171]  3.2.2 (Ensure personnel trained in security responsibilities); 3.2.3 (Provide security awareness training)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Phishing simulation reports (quarterly) including click rates, credential-entry rates, and report rates; remedial training completion records for failed participants; trend analysis showing improvement over time$p$, $p$Conduct phishing simulations at least quarterly. Track and report click rates and credential-entry rates per campaign. Provide immediate remedial training to staff who fail. Report simulation trends and remediation outcomes to management. Adjust simulation complexity based on results.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s4.q32$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$CLOUD SECURITY & VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS$p$, 33, $p$Has the organisation conducted a Cloud Security Assessment for all cloud platforms (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) storing or processing personal information, covering shared responsibility, data residency, and security configuration?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19; s.72 (Transborder information flows) | ISO 27701 cl.6.12; ISO 27002 cl.5.23 (Information security for use of cloud services); CSA CCM$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Cloud security assessment report per platform; shared responsibility matrix (per cloud provider); data residency confirmation (data stored in South Africa or approved jurisdiction); cloud security configuration benchmark results (CIS Benchmark, AWS Well-Architected, Azure Security Benchmark)$p$, $p$Conduct a cloud security assessment for each cloud platform used to process personal information. Confirm data residency aligns with POPIA s.72 (transborder flow restrictions). Review and configure against CIS Benchmarks or the provider's security baseline. Document the shared responsibility model. Reassess annually and after any significant configuration change.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q33$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$CLOUD SECURITY & VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS$p$, 34, $p$Are cloud environments configured according to a secure baseline (e.g., CIS Benchmark, provider security baseline), with publicly exposed storage (S3 buckets, Azure Blob, Google Cloud Storage) actively monitored and access-restricted?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.5.23, cl.8.20 (Networks security); CIS Benchmarks$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) tool reports (e.g., Defender for Cloud, AWS Security Hub, Prisma Cloud); public access block configuration evidence for all storage buckets; infrastructure-as-code (IaC) security scanning results; misconfiguration remediation log$p$, $p$Deploy a Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) solution for all cloud environments. Enable public access block on all storage services (S3, Azure Blob). Conduct weekly CSPM scans and remediate critical findings within 14 days. Include cloud environments in the monthly vulnerability management programme.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q34$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$CLOUD SECURITY & VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS$p$, 35, $p$Is access to cloud management consoles and administrative functions restricted to authorised personnel, protected by MFA, and subject to privileged access management (PAM) controls equivalent to on-premises systems?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.8.2 (Privileged access rights), cl.8.5 (Secure authentication); ISO 27701 cl.6.7$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Cloud IAM (Identity and Access Management) configuration; list of cloud admin accounts with MFA confirmation; Conditional Access / SCP (Service Control Policies) configuration; Just-In-Time (JIT) access records for privileged cloud actions; cloud access review evidence$p$, $p$Apply least-privilege IAM policies across all cloud platforms. Enforce MFA for all cloud console access. Implement Just-In-Time (JIT) privileged access for cloud administrative functions. Conduct quarterly cloud IAM access reviews. Disable or remove dormant accounts within 30 days.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q35$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$CRYPTOGRAPHIC KEY MANAGEMENT$p$, 36, $p$Does the organisation have a Cryptographic Key Management Policy defining key generation, storage, rotation, revocation, and destruction standards for all encryption keys protecting personal information?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.8.24 (Use of cryptography); ISO 27001 Annex A.8.24; NIST SP 800-57$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Cryptographic Key Management Policy; key inventory (all keys protecting PI, algorithm, key length, rotation schedule, custodian); Hardware Security Module (HSM) or key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault) configuration evidence; key rotation log$p$, $p$Develop and implement a Cryptographic Key Management Policy. Maintain a key inventory for all keys protecting personal information. Use a dedicated Key Management System (KMS) or HSM for key storage. Define key rotation schedules (at minimum annually for symmetric keys). Implement dual-control and split-knowledge for high-value keys. Document key destruction procedures.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q36$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$CRYPTOGRAPHIC KEY MANAGEMENT$p$, 37, $p$Are encryption keys for personal information stored separately from the data they protect, with access to key management systems restricted, logged, and subject to privileged access controls?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.8.24; NIST SP 800-57 Part 1$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Key storage configuration evidence (KMS, HSM, or encrypted key store separate from data); access control list for key management system; key access audit logs; evidence that keys are not stored in plaintext alongside encrypted data$p$, $p$Ensure all encryption keys are stored in a dedicated, access-controlled key management system or HSM — never co-located with the data they protect. Restrict key access to named custodians. Enable key access audit logging. Review key access logs monthly for anomalies.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q37$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$SECURITY TESTING & ASSURANCE$p$, 38, $p$Are security configuration reviews (hardening assessments) conducted for all new systems and major changes to systems processing personal information before they are promoted to production?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.8.8, cl.8.31 (Separation of development, test and production environments)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Security configuration review checklist; hardening standards (CIS Benchmark, DISA STIG); pre-production security sign-off records; change management records linking security review to system changes; security-by-design documentation for new systems$p$, $p$Implement a security sign-off requirement in the change management and project delivery processes. Require a security configuration review against an approved hardening standard before any new system or material change goes live. Document and retain sign-off evidence. Integrate security review into the SDLC.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q38$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$SECURITY TESTING & ASSURANCE$p$, 39, $p$Is application security testing (SAST, DAST, or web application penetration testing) conducted for all web applications and APIs that process personal information, with findings remediated within defined SLAs?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.8.25–8.29 (Secure development); OWASP Top 10$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Application penetration test reports (annual minimum); DAST/SAST scan results and remediation logs; OWASP Top 10 assessment results; critical/high finding remediation evidence within agreed SLA (critical: 14 days, high: 30 days); SDLC security policy$p$, $p$Conduct application security testing (web app penetration test or DAST) at least annually for all web applications processing personal information. Integrate SAST into the CI/CD pipeline. Define and enforce remediation SLAs: critical within 14 days, high within 30 days. Track open findings in the vulnerability register. Report to management quarterly.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q39$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$SECURITY TESTING & ASSURANCE$p$, 40, $p$Is a formal Vulnerability and Patch Management programme in place that covers all systems processing personal information, with defined SLAs for patching based on CVSS severity, and tracked to closure?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.8.8 (Management of technical vulnerabilities)$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Vulnerability management policy; monthly scan reports covering all in-scope assets; patch compliance dashboard (% patched within SLA by severity); patch SLA definition (Critical: 14 days, High: 30 days, Medium: 90 days, Low: risk-accepted); exception register with compensating controls$p$, $p$Establish a formal vulnerability management programme. Scan all in-scope systems monthly. Define patch SLAs aligned to CVSS: Critical ≤14 days, High ≤30 days, Medium ≤90 days. Track patching compliance and report monthly to the IO. Document exceptions with risk acceptance and compensating controls. Include cloud, on-premises, and endpoint assets.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q40$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$INFORMATION CLASSIFICATION & LABELLING$p$, 41, $p$Does the organisation have a documented Information Classification Policy defining classification levels (at minimum: Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) with specific categories for personal information and special categories of personal information?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$[POPIA]  s.19 (Security safeguards); s.26 (Special categories of PI require heightened controls)
[ISO 27001]  A.5.12 (Classification of information); A.5.13 (Labelling of information)
[ISO 27002]  cl.5.12 (Classification of information); cl.5.13 (Labelling of information)
[CIS Controls v8 IG1]  CIS 3.2 — Establish and Maintain a Data Inventory; CIS 3.3 — Configure Data Access Control Lists
[NIST CSF 2.0]  ID.AM-05 (Resources inventoried per classification); PR.DS-01 (Data-at-rest protected per classification); GV.OC-05 (Outcomes, capabilities, services delivered per classification)
[Cyber Essentials]  CE Control 3 — Access Control: users only access data they need (classification supports least privilege implementation)
[NIST SP 800-171]  3.1.3 (Control flow of CUI in accordance with approved authorisations); 3.13.16 (Protect CUI at rest)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Information Classification Policy; classification taxonomy document; data classification examples per category; evidence of classification levels being applied in document management systems (SharePoint labels, Microsoft Purview, etc.)$p$, $p$Develop and communicate an Information Classification Policy. Define at minimum four levels: Public, Internal, Confidential, and Restricted. Map personal information to Confidential and special categories of PI to Restricted. Implement labelling in document management and email systems. Train all staff on classification obligations.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s4.q41$p$),
  (4, $p$Security of Personal Information$p$, $p$INFORMATION CLASSIFICATION & LABELLING$p$, 42, $p$Is personal information consistently labelled and classified within systems, documents, and communications, and are handling controls (printing restrictions, email encryption, external sharing controls) applied based on classification level?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.19 | ISO 27002 cl.5.12, cl.5.13; ISO 27701 cl.6.5 (PII access); Microsoft Purview / equivalent$p$, $p$Medium$p$, $p$Classification label deployment evidence (Purview, AIP, or equivalent); DLP policy rules mapped to classification labels; email restriction rules for Confidential/Restricted data; SharePoint/OneDrive external sharing configuration; classification compliance scan reports$p$, $p$Deploy automated classification labels integrated with DLP policies. Configure email to warn or block external sharing of Confidential/Restricted content. Restrict printing of Restricted documents to approved devices. Conduct quarterly classification compliance scans. Identify and remediate unclassified data stores processing personal information.$p$, $p$large$p$, $p$popia.s4.q42$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$GOVERNANCE & DESIGNATED RESPONSIBILITY$p$, 1, $p$Has the organisation designated responsibility for managing all data subject rights requests to the Information Officer (or a deputy), and are the IO's contact details published and accessible to data subjects?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.55–56; s.23–25 | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.1 (Obligations to PII principals), cl.5.3.2$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$IO appointment letter; IO contact details on website and PAIA Manual; data subject rights request intake form/email address; organisational chart$p$, $p$Ensure the IO's contact details are prominently published on the website, in the PAIA Manual, and in all privacy notices. Create a dedicated data subject rights intake channel (email or web form). Designate a deputy IO to manage requests during IO absence.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q01$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$RIGHT OF ACCESS (DSAR)$p$, 2, $p$Does the organisation have a documented procedure for receiving, verifying identity for, logging, processing, and responding to data subject access requests (DSARs) within the 30-day statutory period?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.23 (Right of access); PAIA s.51 | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.2 (Providing access to PII), cl.7.3.6$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$DSAR procedure document; DSAR tracking register (date received, due date, status, outcome); response templates; identity verification checklist; evidence of responses within 30 days$p$, $p$Develop a DSAR procedure covering: intake, identity verification (without requesting excessive information), acknowledgement within 5 days, information search across all systems, response drafting, IO approval, and dispatch within 30 days. Set Day-20 and Day-27 reminder alerts.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q02$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$RIGHT OF ACCESS (DSAR)$p$, 3, $p$Is the identity of the requestor verified before any personal information is disclosed, using a proportionate verification process that does not create an unnecessary barrier to exercising rights?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.23 | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.6 (Providing mechanism to obtain PII)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Identity verification procedure; proportionality assessment for verification requirements per data sensitivity; verification checklist; log of verification steps taken per DSAR$p$, $p$Define a proportionate identity verification process: for low-risk requests, confirmation of name and email address may suffice; for high-risk or large-volume requests, require government-issued ID. Do not request more information than necessary for verification. Document the process and train all DSAR handlers.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q03$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$RIGHT TO CORRECTION & DELETION$p$, 4, $p$Does the organisation have a documented procedure for handling requests to correct inaccurate or incomplete personal information, with corrections actioned across all relevant systems within 30 days?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.24 (Correction of personal information) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.4 (Correcting or erasing PII)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Correction request procedure; correction request log; evidence of corrections applied in all in-scope systems; notifications sent to third parties where data was previously disclosed$p$, $p$Develop a correction request procedure. On receipt of a correction request, update all systems containing the inaccurate data within 30 days. Where data has been disclosed to third parties, notify them of the correction. Log all correction requests and outcomes.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q04$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$RIGHT TO CORRECTION & DELETION$p$, 5, $p$Does the organisation have a procedure for deleting or de-identifying personal information on request where retention is no longer lawfully required, and is this actioned within 30 days?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.24(3); s.14 | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.4 (Correcting or erasing PII), cl.7.4.8 (Disposal of PII)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Deletion request procedure; deletion request log; deletion confirmation to data subject; evidence of removal from all systems (including backups where feasible); exceptions log where legal retention obligation applies$p$, $p$Develop a deletion/erasure request procedure. Assess whether a legal retention obligation overrides the deletion request. Where deletion is required, action across all systems including CRM, HR, ERP, and archives within 30 days. Provide written confirmation to the data subject.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q05$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$RIGHT TO OBJECT & RESTRICT PROCESSING$p$, 6, $p$Does the organisation have a procedure for handling objections to processing under POPIA s.11(3) and s.25, including suspension of processing pending review?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.11(3); s.25 (Right to object to processing) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.5 (Providing mechanism to object to PII processing)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Objection handling procedure; objection request log; evidence of processing suspension during review; IO decision record; communication to data subject of outcome$p$, $p$Develop an objection handling procedure. On receipt of an objection, suspend the relevant processing pending IO review. Assess whether compelling legitimate grounds override the objection. Communicate the decision (with reasons) to the data subject within 30 days.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q06$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$RIGHT TO LODGE A COMPLAINT$p$, 7, $p$Are data subjects informed of their right to lodge a complaint with the Information Regulator, and is there an internal complaints procedure that provides a first-line resolution mechanism?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.74 (Complaints to the Information Regulator) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.1, cl.7.3.9 (Automated decision-making)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Privacy notice referencing right to complain to IR; internal complaints procedure; complaint register; evidence of IR complaint referral process$p$, $p$Include in all privacy notices the data subject's right to lodge a complaint with the Information Regulator (www.inforegulator.org.za). Establish an internal first-line complaints procedure with a target response time of 20 business days. Maintain a complaint register.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q07$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$AUTOMATED DECISION-MAKING$p$, 8, $p$Where the organisation uses automated decision-making (including profiling) that produces legal or similarly significant effects on data subjects, has this been identified and are appropriate safeguards in place?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.71 (Automated processing) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.9 (Automated decision-making)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Inventory of automated decision-making processes; privacy notice disclosure of automated processing; data subject right to human review procedure; PIA for high-risk automated decisions$p$, $p$Identify all automated decision-making and profiling activities. Disclose these in the applicable privacy notice. Establish a process for data subjects to request human review of automated decisions. Conduct a PIA for any high-risk automated decision-making activity.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q08$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$STAFF AWARENESS & REQUEST TRACKING$p$, 9, $p$Are front-line staff trained to recognise data subject rights requests (regardless of how they are submitted — verbal, written, social media) and to route them correctly to the IO within one business day?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.23–25 | ISO 27701 cl.7.4 (Awareness), cl.7.3 (Obligations to PII principals)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Staff training records on data subject rights; call/email scripts for routing requests; DSAR routing procedure; evidence of requests correctly identified and routed$p$, $p$Include data subject rights request recognition and routing in all POPIA training. Provide call scripts for customer-facing staff. Require that any suspected DSAR is escalated to the IO within one business day, regardless of the channel received.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q09$p$),
  (5, $p$Data Subject Rights & Requests$p$, $p$STAFF AWARENESS & REQUEST TRACKING$p$, 10, $p$Are all data subject rights requests logged in a centralised tracking system, with automated reminders to ensure responses are dispatched within the 30-day statutory period?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.23–25 | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.6$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$DSAR tracking register or ticketing system (ServiceNow, Jira, OneTrust, etc.); automated reminder evidence (Day 20, Day 27 alerts); monthly DSAR metric report to IO; escalation log for complex requests$p$, $p$Implement a centralised DSAR tracking register (or dedicated ticketing system). Configure automated reminders at Day 20 and Day 27. Report DSAR volumes, completion rates, and average response times to the IO monthly. Escalate any at-risk requests to senior management.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s5.q10$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$CONSENT MANAGEMENT$p$, 1, $p$Does the organisation obtain explicit, freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous opt-in consent from data subjects before conducting any direct marketing by electronic means (email, SMS, WhatsApp, VOIP, fax, automated calls)?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69 (Unsolicited electronic communications); s.11(1)(a) | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.3 (Consent), cl.7.3.3$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Consent management system or CRM consent records (timestamp, consent wording version, channel, purpose); opt-in forms/checkboxes (no pre-ticked boxes); consent audit report$p$, $p$Implement an opt-in consent mechanism for every electronic marketing channel. Ensure consent is granular (per channel and per purpose), unambiguous (opt-in, not opt-out), and records include the exact wording accepted, channel, timestamp, and data source. Pre-ticked boxes or bundled consent are non-compliant.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q01$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$CONSENT MANAGEMENT$p$, 2, $p$Is consent for direct marketing captured separately from consent for other processing purposes (no bundled consent)?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.11(1)(a); s.69 | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.3 (When and how consent is obtained)$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Consent form design evidence (separate checkboxes for marketing vs. service purposes); CRM configuration showing distinct consent flags per purpose; consent wording review log$p$, $p$Audit all consent collection mechanisms. Ensure marketing consent is a separate, optional tick-box from consent for service delivery or contractual purposes. Update web forms, paper forms, and call scripts to separate marketing consent.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q02$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$CONSENT MANAGEMENT$p$, 3, $p$Are consent records retained for the duration of the marketing relationship and for a minimum of 3 years thereafter as proof of consent?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.14; s.69 | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.3, cl.7.4.7$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Consent record retention policy; CRM export showing consent date, wording, channel; evidence that historical consent records are preserved even after opt-out$p$, $p$Configure the CRM or consent management platform to retain consent records (date, wording version, channel) for the duration of the relationship plus 3 years. Consent records must be preserved even after a data subject opts out, as evidence of the prior lawful marketing.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q03$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$OPT-OUT & CONSENT WITHDRAWAL$p$, 4, $p$Is a simple, cost-free opt-out / unsubscribe mechanism included in every direct marketing communication, and is the opt-out actioned within 5 business days?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69(3)(b) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.5 (Providing mechanism to modify or withdraw consent)$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Sample email, SMS, and digital marketing communications showing opt-out link/instruction; opt-out processing log with action dates; suppression list; SLA evidence$p$, $p$Ensure every electronic marketing communication includes a one-click unsubscribe link (email) or STOP instruction (SMS). Define an opt-out SLA of 5 business days. Configure the CRM to automatically suppress opted-out data subjects. Conduct monthly checks to confirm suppression.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q04$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$OPT-OUT & CONSENT WITHDRAWAL$p$, 5, $p$Is the suppression/do-not-contact list maintained centrally and scrubbed against all marketing lists before every campaign send?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69(3) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.5$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Suppression list management procedure; pre-campaign scrubbing evidence (list counts before and after suppression); CRM suppression list report; evidence of cross-channel suppression (email, SMS, phone)$p$, $p$Maintain a centralised suppression list covering all marketing channels. Require mandatory list scrubbing immediately before every campaign send. Confirm suppression across all channels simultaneously (a data subject who opts out of email must also be suppressed from SMS if they have not separately consented).$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q05$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$THIRD-PARTY & OUTSOURCED MARKETING$p$, 6, $p$Where marketing activities are outsourced to agencies or platforms, is a POPIA-compliant Data Processing Agreement (DPA / Operator Agreement) in place with each, and have they been assessed for compliance?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.20–22 (Operators); s.69 | ISO 27701 cl.6.12.1 (Supplier relationships), cl.8.5$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Signed DPAs with all marketing operators; operator compliance assessment questionnaire; ISO 27001/27701 certification or equivalent; right-to-audit clause in DPA; annual review records$p$, $p$Identify all marketing operators (agencies, email service providers, CRM platforms, ad networks). Ensure a POPIA-compliant DPA is signed with each before any personal information is shared. Conduct an annual compliance assessment. Exercise the right to audit for high-risk operators at least every two years.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q06$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$THIRD-PARTY & OUTSOURCED MARKETING$p$, 7, $p$If the organisation purchases, rents, or uses third-party marketing lists, are robust due diligence steps taken to verify that valid, specific consent exists for data subjects to be contacted by the organisation?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69; s.11(1)(a) | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.3$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$List provider contracts including consent warranty; sample consent evidence from list provider; due diligence checklist; organisation's own re-consent campaign records where provenance of consent is uncertain$p$, $p$Require written consent warranties from all list providers, confirming that data subjects have given valid, specific consent to receive marketing from your organisation (not merely the list provider). Where consent provenance is uncertain, conduct a re-consent campaign before use. Cease use of any list where valid consent cannot be demonstrated.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q07$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$THIRD-PARTY & OUTSOURCED MARKETING$p$, 8, $p$Does the organisation prohibit sharing or selling customer personal information to third parties for their own marketing purposes without the explicit consent of the data subject?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.11(1)(a); s.69; s.72 | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.7 (Disclosures to third parties)$p$, $p$Critical$p$, $p$Data sharing policy; contracts with third parties prohibiting unauthorised data sharing; data subject consent records for any approved sharing; audit log of data sharing events$p$, $p$Implement a data sharing policy that prohibits the sale or transfer of customer personal information to third parties for independent marketing purposes without explicit, separate consent. Audit data sharing activities annually. Include data sharing restrictions in all contracts with partners.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q08$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$ELECTRONIC MARKETING COMPLIANCE$p$, 9, $p$Do all electronic marketing communications identify the organisation by full legal name, include a physical or postal address, and provide a valid contact address, as required by POPIA s.69(3)(a) and ECTA s.45?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69(3)(a) | ECTA s.45; ISO 27701 cl.7.3.2$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Sample marketing emails, SMSs, and digital communications; marketing template compliance review log; ECTA s.45 compliance checklist$p$, $p$Review all electronic marketing templates. Ensure each communication includes: the organisation's full legal name, a valid physical or postal address, and a valid reply-to email or telephone number. Update non-compliant templates. Conduct a quarterly template review.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q09$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$ELECTRONIC MARKETING COMPLIANCE$p$, 10, $p$Is the organisation's use of electronic marketing channels (email, SMS, WhatsApp, social media) documented in a Direct Marketing Policy that assigns roles, responsibilities, approved channels, and compliance requirements?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69 | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.1, cl.5.2 (PIMS policy)$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Direct Marketing Policy (board-approved, version-controlled); channel-specific compliance guidelines; role assignments (marketing manager, IO, compliance team); training records for marketing staff$p$, $p$Develop a Direct Marketing Policy covering: permitted channels, consent requirements per channel, opt-out SLAs, prohibited practices, operator requirements, and the IO's oversight role. Obtain board approval. Train all marketing staff on the policy annually.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q10$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$PROFILING & TARGETED MARKETING$p$, 11, $p$Where the organisation uses profiling or behavioural targeting for marketing purposes (including through cookies, tracking pixels, or third-party data platforms), is this disclosed in the privacy notice and are appropriate consent mechanisms in place?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.69; s.71 (Automated processing) | ISO 27701 cl.7.3.9 (Automated decision-making); ECTA$p$, $p$High$p$, $p$Privacy notice and cookie policy disclosing profiling and targeted marketing; consent management platform (CMP) cookie banner; opt-out mechanism for profiling; PIA for high-risk profiling activities$p$, $p$Disclose all profiling and behavioural targeting activities in the privacy notice and cookie policy. Implement a POPIA-compliant consent management platform for cookies and tracking technologies. Provide a clear opt-out from profiling for marketing purposes. Conduct a PIA for any high-risk profiling activity.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q11$p$),
  (6, $p$Direct Marketing$p$, $p$PROFILING & TARGETED MARKETING$p$, 12, $p$Does the organisation maintain records of all direct marketing campaigns (channel, data subjects targeted, consent basis, suppression applied, opt-out rate) to demonstrate accountability under POPIA?$p$, $p$$p$, $p$POPIA s.55 (Accountability) | ISO 27701 cl.7.2.1 (RoPA), cl.5.1 (Leadership and commitment)$p$, $p$Medium$p$, $p$Campaign records log (date, channel, audience size, consent basis, suppression applied, opt-out count); campaign compliance sign-off checklist; annual marketing compliance report to IO$p$, $p$Implement a campaign records register capturing: campaign date, channel, number of data subjects targeted, consent basis relied upon, suppression list applied, opt-out count, and post-campaign compliance sign-off. Retain records for a minimum of 3 years. Report marketing compliance metrics to the IO quarterly.$p$, $p$all$p$, $p$popia.s6.q12$p$),
